   "kind": "core",
   "title": "Plate with a central circular hole in tension: stress concentration, volume-preserving voxel fill (voxelizer: centre)",
   "reference": "Kirsch (1898) for an infinite plate: the hoop stress at the edge of a circular hole in uniaxial tension is 3 sigma (Timoshenko and Goodier, Theory of Elasticity, section 58). For a finite width plate the peak is Kt_net sigma_net with Kt_net = 3.00 - 3.13 (d/W) + 3.66 (d/W)^2 - 1.53 (d/W)^3 (Peterson's Stress Concentration Factors / Roark's Formulas for Stress and Strain, plate with a central hole in tension; recalled, not re-checked against the book) and sigma_net = sigma W/(W - d). Here d 6 mm, W 24 mm (d/W 0.25): Kt_net 2.4223, peak = 3.2298 x 100 MPa = 322.98 MPa at the hole edge on the symmetry plane normal to the load. A quarter of the plate (30 x 12 x 1 mm, hole radius 3 mm, voxel 0.25 mm = a/12) with sliders on the three symmetry planes and 100 MPa tension at the far end. The spec asks for the recovered stress within 10 % (after smoothing over 2 voxels).",
